# ScheduleSolve AI — environment template.
#
# Copy this file to `.env` and fill in your key. NEVER commit `.env` — it is
# gitignored. This file is the only one that should ever be in the repo.
#
# Get a key at the Meta Model API dashboard: https://dev.meta.ai/dashboard

# --- Required: your Meta Model API key (this is what enables Muse Spark) ------
# MODEL_API_KEY and MUSE_API_KEY are both accepted; MODEL_API_KEY wins.
MODEL_API_KEY=your-meta-model-api-key-here

# --- Optional: model + endpoint overrides (defaults shown) --------------------
# Contributor mode = discounted; content may train future Meta models. Fine for
# synthetic demo data. Use "muse-spark-1.3" (Standard) for real institutional
# data so no staff data ever trains Meta's models.
MUSE_MODEL=muse-spark-1.3-contributor
MUSE_BASE_URL=https://api.meta.ai/v1
MUSE_REASONING_EFFORT=low

# --- Optional: open-weights Muse Spark Open instead of the hosted API --------
# The client is endpoint-agnostic. To run the same roles on Muse Spark Open
# served by Baseten / Modal / local vLLM, point the two vars above at that
# endpoint, e.g.:
#   MUSE_BASE_URL=https://inference.baseten.co/v1
#   MUSE_MODEL=meta-models/muse-spark-open-1026
#   MODEL_API_KEY=<that provider's key>
# Structured output must be served by the endpoint; verify before relying on
# it:  cd backend && .venv/bin/python scripts/verify_muse.py

# --- Muse Voice Transcribe (hosted Meta ASR) -----------------------------------
# ASR is not an OpenAI-compatible chat model. When chat runs on Baseten / vLLM,
# keep the voice note call on the Meta Model API:
# MUSE_ASR_BASE_URL=https://api.meta.ai/v1
# MUSE_ASR_API_KEY=<your Meta Model API key>
# MUSE_ASR_MODEL=muse-voice-transcribe-1.0
# Defaults: base URL above, key = MUSE_API_KEY, model above.

# --- Database ------------------------------------------------------------------
# Postgres 18 runs in Docker. Compose sets DATABASE_URL automatically to
# postgresql://schedulesolve:schedulesolve@db:5432/schedulesolve.
# For a backend running on the host against the Compose database:
# DATABASE_URL=postgresql://schedulesolve:schedulesolve@localhost:5433/schedulesolve
# The Docker stack requires Postgres. The hosted single-tenant demo instead runs
# SQLite on a volume (ALLOW_SQLITE=1); both use the same SQLAlchemy models.

# --- Optional: public-demo cost guard -----------------------------------------
# Weighted daily model budget for the keyless demo; exhaustion returns 429 and
# degrades to the labelled deterministic fallback.
# DEMO_DAILY_MODEL_BUDGET=300

# --- Optional: ports (used by ./start.sh and docker-compose) -------------------
# BACKEND_PORT=8000
# FRONTEND_PORT=3000
# PG_PORT=5433
# --- Optional: model call timeout ---------------------------------------------
# Seconds before a single Muse call is abandoned and the role falls back to the
# labelled deterministic path.
# MUSE_TIMEOUT_S=120

# --- Optional: solver budget and workers --------------------------------------
# CP-SAT gets this many seconds per solve before it reports `unknown`. The
# 361-section stress tier is where that matters: CP-SAT exhausts the budget
# while the native C++ core proves the optimum in ~5.6 s. Demo scales finish in
# milliseconds, so a larger budget costs nothing there.
# SOLVER_TIME_LIMIT_S=60
# HOME_SOLVER_TIME_LIMIT_S=30
# SOLVER_WORKERS=1

# --- SQLite opt-in — hosted demo only -----------------------------------------
# This project REQUIRES Postgres 18. `backend/app/db.py` refuses to start on
# SQLite unless ALLOW_SQLITE=1, so no local or CI run can silently fall back to
# a file database. The hosted single-tenant demo opts in explicitly, because a
# zero-setup judge URL cannot ship a database service:
#   DATABASE_URL=sqlite:////data/schedulesolve.db
#   ALLOW_SQLITE=1
# Same SQLAlchemy models, same schema, same API — only the engine differs.
